## Method and reasoning
Each subset has exactly one bitmask. Visit masks numerically and inspect bits from least to most significant to preserve input order. Print size before elements so even the empty subset has a token.

## Walk through the sample
For [8,−3], masks 0,1,2,3 produce the empty subset, [8], [−3], [8,−3]. Bit zero refers to the first input position, so sorting the values would change the required order.

## Complexity
Time O(n·2ⁿ), auxiliary space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<int> a(n);
    for (int &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    for (unsigned mask = 0; mask < (1u << n); ++mask) {
        cout << popcount(mask);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            if (mask >> i & 1u)
                cout << ' ' << a[i];
        cout << '\n';
    }
}
~~~
